.DS_Store
# cinemagraph raw pre-loop clips — kept locally so a loop can be switched (boomerang↔crossfade) without
# re-generating; the player never needs them, so don't push them to the public site.
**/*_raw.mp4
# the UNPATCHED render a clip is rebuilt from when a repair patch is toggled off in the gallery.
# Local-only like the raws: the player fetches the baked cine_<state>.mp4 and nothing else.
**/*_src.mp4
**/*.reloop.mp4
# pre-edit cinemagraph originals (the picked/played clip keeps the plain name)
**/*_orig.mp4
# seam-repair undo snapshots — every gradient/AI seam fix drops one beside the image it repaired, and
# they MUST stay on disk (that is what the hub's Undo reads). The player never fetches them. 19 of these
# were sitting in Egypt alone at its audit (2026-08-28).
**/*_undo*.png
# the world plate — the shared continuity REFERENCE the art pipeline feeds to gpt-image, shown in the
# console's World-plate panel. Authoring-only: it is never fetched at play time, but it must stay on disk
# (moving it breaks both the panel and the generation reference), so ignore it rather than archiving it.
**/_world/

# --- Keep the public repo to PLAY-REQUIRED assets only (2026-08-06) ---
# Rule: only files the player fetches at runtime (scenario.json, play.html, referenced
# panoramas/cinemagraphs/audio/images, data CSVs, shared engine) belong on the
# public site. NOT decoder/ — it holds the answer keys and is ignored below.
# Authoring records, build caches, backups, and history-tier dirs do not.
# NOTE: .gitignore does NOT untrack already-committed files — after this syncs to the Mac,
# run once in the escape_rooms repo:  git rm -r --cached . && git add . && git commit
# (re-applies these rules; disk files are untouched, git just stops tracking the ignored ones.)

# Design records + authoring docs (root/**/AGENTS.md already ignored above)
**/notes.md
**/wiring_checklist.md
# the vetted landing-story snapshot validate_story.py diffs against — authoring only
**/.vetted_story.txt
# All backup / pre-edit snapshots (broadens the scenario.json.bak line above)
**/*.bak
**/scenario.json.pre_*
# Python build/test caches
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
# ALL history-tier archive dirs (the z_ convention), not just z_history/
z_*/
**/z_*/


# --- Cinemagraph R&D bench — authoring-only (2026-08-31) ---
# authoring_v2/ui/cine360/ is the 360-cinemagraph parameter-sweep bench: ~325 experiment clips,
# 2.1 GB, several over GitHub's 100 MB hard limit. No scenario.json references it. It wedged a
# push once; githooks/pre-push now catches that class. Ignore the media AND the bench pages.
authoring_v2/ui/cine360/
authoring_v2/ui/cine360_*.html

# Timestamped / suffixed backups — `**/*.bak` above does NOT match `*.bak_20260830_0748`,
# `*.bak_flash_1022`, `*.bak_20260828` etc., which is how four cine360_test snapshots and a
# scenario.json backup reached the same commit.
**/*.bak_*
**/*.pre_*

# Authoring records + scratch (canon: only play-required files reach public GitHub)
notes/
temp/

# pre-art gate stamp — authoring artefact, never published
.preflight_ok


# --- Publish PLAY-REQUIRED only: the whole-directory pass (2026-09-04, Lucas) ---
# The 2026-08-06 rule above ("only files the player fetches at runtime belong on the public site") was
# applied to authoring RECORDS but never to the authoring TOOLING, so five directories were still being
# published. Verified before ignoring: nothing in any `play.html`, `scenario.json` or `shared/*` fetches
# these at runtime — the only references are prose comments and designNotes.
#
# KEPT (do not add here):
#   rooms/    shared/    — what the player actually loads
#   githooks/ — 8 KB of repo infrastructure, not play content, but a fresh clone needs it to install the
#               oversize-blob pre-push guard (`ln -sf ../../githooks/pre-push .git/hooks/pre-push`).
#               Untracking it would break that documented step.
#
# NOTE (same caveat as the rules above): .gitignore does NOT untrack already-committed files. These stay
# published until you run ONCE on the Mac, in the escape_rooms repo:
#     git rm -r --cached .     then re-stage the tree (git add .) and commit
# Disk files are untouched; git just stops tracking the ignored ones. Adding the rules alone changes
# nothing live, so this can sit here safely until you choose to run it.
#
# TRADEOFF, stated plainly: this repo is the ONLY git history for these paths (the Linux box has no git
# here). Untracking stops FUTURE changes being versioned; past commits keep their history. Syncthing still
# carries the files to both machines, so they are not at risk of loss, only of being unversioned.
authoring_v2/
cinemagraph_tools/
consistency/
decoder/
tests/

# PER-SCENARIO TEST SUITES. `rooms/<chapter>/<scenario>/test_<name>.py` re-derives every rung's answer
# from the CSV, so each one spells its scenario's answers out in plain text — subway's names all four
# genes in its module docstring. They are authoring/CI files: nothing in any `play.html`, `scenario.json`
# or `shared/*` fetches them, and `run_all_tests.py` finds them on DISK, not over HTTP. They were being
# published: all thirteen were live and fetchable on 2026-09-25 (verified with curl, every one a 200),
# because the 2026-09-04 "publish play-required only" pass ignored the authoring DIRECTORIES but never
# the test files that sit inside `rooms/`. `tests/` and `authoring_v2/` were already covered; this is
# the third home.
# NOTE (as everywhere in this file): .gitignore does NOT untrack an already-committed file. Clearing the
# whole backlog at once — this block, the five ignored root directories, subway's answer-key map and the
# 2026-09-25 third pass below — is ONE command on the Mac, in the escape_rooms repo (the same re-apply
# incantation already written at the top of this file):
#     git rm -r --cached . && git add . && git commit -m "publish play-required files only"
# Verified 2026-09-25 against the LIVE GitHub tree before these rules were written: it removes 639 files
# (121 MB) and leaves 791 — media, data CSVs, scenario.json, play.html, shared/, the audio CREDITS files
# and two infrastructure files. The only play-SHAPED file it removes is subway's network_map.png, which
# is an answer key and is meant to go. Disk files are untouched; the next deploy's bulk staging carries
# the deletions to the live site.
rooms/**/test_*.py

# --- PUBLISH PLAY-REQUIRED ONLY: the third pass (2026-09-25, Lucas) ---
# The 2026-08-06 and 2026-09-04 passes covered authoring RECORDS and authoring DIRECTORIES at the repo
# root. Neither reached the authoring output that accumulates INSIDE `rooms/`, so an audit against the
# live GitHub tree found 471 more files (118 MB) still published, none of them fetched by any player.
# Everything below was checked against every `scenario.json`, `play.html` and `shared/*.js` first.

# ALL PYTHON UNDER rooms/. The player's engine is WebR — it runs R, and fetches no Python, ever. So
# every .py here is authoring: dataset builders, figure/tile/seal makers, `_motion_specs.py`,
# waterfalls' `cave_engine/` package, and the per-scenario `test_*.py` (which get their own block below
# because they leak ANSWERS, a sharper problem than footprint).
#
# THE REGENERATORS STAY WHERE THEY ARE — they are NOT moved to `_scratch/` (Lucas, 2026-09-25, having
# asked whether moving would break anything: it would). Every one of them anchors on
# `os.path.dirname(__file__)` and five also walk `../`, so relocating a script one directory deeper
# silently changes where it reads and writes — wrong-output-path failures, not errors. And the
# versioning outcome is identical either way: in this repo, unpublished means unversioned, because the
# git history lives only on the Mac. Ignoring them in place keeps them working, keeps Syncthing
# carrying them to both machines, and costs nothing that moving them would have saved.
rooms/**/*.py

# AGENT + PROBE WORKING DIRECTORIES. `_long_agent/` is room_iterate's run state (plan, progress,
# per-cycle evaluations and decisions, render logs) and `_motion_probe*/` are motion parameter sweeps.
# 238 files, 118 MB — nearly the whole of the overrun. The root AGENTS.md left `_long_agent/` explicitly
# undecided ("whether it belongs in .gitignore beside _scratch/ is undecided"); it is decided here.
# NOTE they are invisible on the WEBSITE either way — GitHub Pages will not serve a path segment
# starting with `_` — but the repo is PUBLIC, so they are browsable on github.com. That accident is
# probably why this went unnoticed; it is not a safeguard to rely on.
**/_long_agent/
**/_motion_probe*/

# PER-ROOM AUTHORING RECORDS dropped beside the art they describe: the art judge's verdict on a still
# (`scene.judge.json`, `cine_<state>.judge.json`), the cinemagraph candidate ledger (`*.cand.json`),
# the repair-mask and patch records (`*.mask.json`, `*.patches.json`), the publish ledger
# (`.cine_published.json`), and the scene-spec bundle (authoring INPUT — the engine reads
# scenario.json, which the spec is compiled into).
# NB: gitignore has NO trailing comments — a `#` only starts a comment at the START of a line. These
# five patterns were written with trailing comments first time round and silently matched nothing,
# which the check-ignore pass against the live tree caught. Keep comments on their own lines.
**/*.judge.json
**/*.cand.json
**/*.mask.json
**/*.patches.json
**/.cine_published.json
**/scene_specs_*.json

# AUTHORING PAGES living in a scenario directory. Verified unreferenced by any `play.html` or
# `scenario.json`: waterfalls' `puzzle_editor.html` is a visual authoring tool, and its
# `player_map.html` was demoted to authoring-only on 2026-09-20 when it stopped being a player hotspot.
**/puzzle_editor.html
**/player_map.html

# The scenario inventory is authoring output (scenario_inventory.py writes it). No runtime consumer:
# nothing in rooms/, shared/ or the surrounding site fetches it.
rooms/scenario_inventory.json

# PLAYWRIGHT OUTPUT outside tests/. `tests/test-results/` was already ignored; two more copies exist
# at the repo root and under shared/, left by a run started from the wrong directory.
**/test-results/

# FOUR ONE-OFF AUTHORING INPUTS, named explicitly rather than by pattern because each is the only file
# of its kind and a broad rule here could later swallow real play data. Each was checked against the
# CURRENT scenario.json, play.html and shared/*.js: none is fetched. The only things that mention them
# are AGENTS.md, notes.md, the builder scripts that write them, the test files, and stale .bak copies.
rooms/data_vis/alaska/escape_grids/grids.json
rooms/data_vis2/airship/nest/codes.json
rooms/flat_clustering/waterfalls/puzzle.json
rooms/hierarchical_clustering/temple/figures/tiles/MANIFEST.json

# DELIBERATELY STILL PUBLISHED: `**/CREDITS.md` (audio attribution). It is a record rather than play
# content, but publishing an attribution file for CC-licensed audio is the point of having one.

# ANSWER-KEY MAP RENDERS. `network_map.png` is subway's answer key — it draws the lines, both ghost
# stations and the marked hops, and a player who sees it has solved the escape. Verified 2026-09-07
# that nothing references it as a played asset (it appears in scenario.json only inside prose design
# notes), so ignoring it costs nothing and closes a "must never ship" by pattern instead of by
# remembering. The PLAYER's sheet is `player_map.png` (same script, `--player`) and DOES ship.
**/network_map.png

# image-model bake-off output. The SCRIPTS in authoring_v2/model_probe/ belong in the repo; their
# RESULTS do not — 416 MB of generated panoramas, which lives in Tools/temp instead (outside every
# repo). This entry is belt-and-braces in case a run is ever pointed back here by accident.
authoring_v2/model_probe/results/
